{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790420733,"nonce":"aav6dN1BRVCXiMotzejTJmz6KLQ=","title":"Compile time of the Cranelift tiers bounds warm-up","message":"method_call (3.1x LuaJIT in CI at 10e8418) is now bounded by compile latency: the module body's baseline compile (3-6 ms on the NUC) and its optimised compile (~8 ms), with the interpreter and baseline code running meanwhile. LuaJIT's whole run is 5-7 ms.\n\nWhere compile-thread time goes (NUC, perf, delta_blue, Cranelift-only, optimising thread): Cranelift excluding regalloc 30%, regalloc2 21%, malloc/free/memmove 15%, our MIR passes 10%, kernel 9%, Cranelift egraph 3%.\n\nWhere the CLIF comes from (instructions emitted per MIR opcode, delta_blue + method_call + nbody + binary_trees): Call 43/op (28.5% of all CLIF), CallKnownFunc 42/op (20.6%), GuardNumAt 75/op (4.6%), GuardClassAt 55/op (4.3%), NewInstance 68/op, SubscriptGet 125/op. Cold blocks are ~16% of CLIF.\n\nMeasured by compile-thread instructions (perf record -e instructions:u -c 1e6, samples on beadie/promoter threads, median of 5; the metric itself moves ~10% between runs because the set of compiles depends on timing):\n- Dropping the error poll after calls entirely (unsafe, measurement only): delta_blue 125M -\u003e 112M, method_call 15M -\u003e 12M, nbody 27M -\u003e 23M.\n- Dropping the current-frame stores (unsafe, measurement only): ~4-5%, near noise.\n- Sharing the poll's leaving block per function plus packing deopt constants into a data object: delta_blue 115M -\u003e 114M, nbody 26M -\u003e 24M, binary_trees 26M -\u003e 25M. Within noise; not committed. (The poll's check block cannot be shared: the lowering uses raw SSA values, so a block reached from every call site breaks dominance.)\n\nSo trimming per-call lowering is worth a few percent at most. Larger levers:\n1. Compile less for run-once code: the module body gets a baseline and an optimised compile; with early top tier (10e8418) the baseline is a fallback only.\n2. Recompile a region (a cold loop's) instead of the whole function.\n3. JIT memory: kernel time is 9% of the compile thread (mmap/mprotect/page faults per compile); pool reservations.\n4. MIR pipeline is 10%: TypeSpecialize's nested fixpoint, UnboxParams and IntSpecialize each ~150-200 us on advance.","files":null}]}